R-BS1 R-BS2 R-ID6 R-P4 R-EF2 R-PH9 R-LD5 R-LD6 R-PP4 R-NS12 R-EF1 R-CC8 (slice 032): the build skip of `Ladder.run!` with the fake probes. 7 runs of `update` on 6 projects (acme/pf 0.1.0, acme/json 2.0.0, acme/parser 1.0.0). The apps import `pf.Std` and `json.Parser` (p4: `parser.Parser`), so the used closure is the platform and json (p4: the platform and parser). `compat.tsv` of the given home has 3 rows for the platform with the target `x64musl`: for the compiler 10-08 a pass and a fail, both with the digest of the used closure of p5 (a hand-made state: the primary key has the digest, so Talon never writes both), and for the compiler 10-07 a pass with no digest.
The calls of a member after the check of the candidate passed, in order (`BuildFlow.before!`, `after!`): `platform` (the target), the scan of the used modules (one `read_files` for each level: the platform module and the json module, none of them exists in the fixture), one `db_query` with the one key (platform hash, compiler, target), then the build probe, or none, and after a pass one `db_write` of the row with the digest of the used closure (`existing=replace`, one row). A skipped build starts no probe (the count in `N probes`).
Run 1: no row: the build runs and its pass is recorded (R-P4). Run 2 (same project, same compiler): parser moves and the app does not import it, so the digest is the same: `build: skipped (recorded pass)`, no build probe, no build write (R-BS2). Run 3 (`--no-probe`, R-EF2): the whole-app check still runs, no unit probe, and the build is skipped. Run 4: the same used closure on another compiler: the key differs, so the build runs and a second row is written (R-NS12, R-ID6). Run 5: parser is used by this app and moves: the digest differs, so the build runs and its pass is a second row of the key: the row of run 1 stays (R-BS2, the primary key has the digest). Run 6: a fail is recorded for the digest of the candidate, with a pass of the same digest: no skip (R-BS2: a recorded fail never allows a skip), the new pass replaces both rows of that digest. Run 7: the row is a pass with no digest: no skip (R-BS1 condition 2).
The digests in `tree.diff` are the SHA-256 of the sorted hashes of the used packages, each with a line end (checked with `sha256sum`: the last column of `compat.tsv`).
